## The work — a checklist over the existing folder

### ➕ Add — `boukensha/prompt_builder.py`

Plain class, five methods, no logic of its own:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `def initialize(context, backend)` | `def __init__(self, context, backend)` — store both |
| `to_messages` | `self.backend.to_messages(self.context.messages)` |
| `to_tools` | `self.backend.to_tools(self.context.tools)` |
| `to_api_payload(max_output_tokens: 1024)` | `def to_api_payload(self, *, max_output_tokens=1024)` → `self.backend.to_payload(self.context, max_output_tokens=max_output_tokens)` |
| `headers` / `url` | `def headers(self)` / `def url(self)` — **methods, not properties**, since `url` is computed per-model on Gemini and step 04 calls them like methods |

> **Port the `to_messages` arity mismatch as-is.** `PromptBuilder#to_messages`
> passes only `messages`, but `OpenAI`, `Ollama`, and `OllamaCloud` define
> `to_messages(system, messages)` — so that delegator raises for three of the
> five backends. This is not a transient bug: the same shape survives unchanged
> through Ruby step 12. `to_api_payload` is the path the example (and every
> later step) uses, and it passes `context.system` correctly. Mirror Ruby, and
> note it in the Python README rather than silently "fixing" it.

### ➕ Add — `boukensha/backends/` (six modules + `__init__.py`)

**`backends/base.py`** ← `base.rb`. A plain class holding the shared model
table logic:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `MODELS` const per subclass, `self.models` reads it via `const_get` | `MODELS: dict = {}` on `Base`; `@classmethod models(cls)` raises `NotImplementedError(f"{cls.__name__} must define MODELS")` when empty |
| `self.model_info(model)` (class) **and** `model_info` (instance) | Python cannot overload — see **OQ 1**; recommended: classmethod `model_info(cls, model)`, instance attribute `self.info` |
| `validate_model!(model)` raising `UnsupportedModelError` | `validate_model(cls, model)` — no `!` suffix in Python; message format per **OQ 2** |
| `models.keys.sort.join(", ")` | `", ".join(sorted(cls.models()))` |
| `configure_model(model)` (private) | `_configure_model(self, model)` — sets `self.model` and `self.info` |
| `model_info.fetch(:context_window)` | `self.info["context_window"]` — `fetch` raises on a missing key, so does `[]` |
| `usage_level` uses `[]` (nil-tolerant) | `self.info.get("usage_level")` — **note the asymmetry with the `fetch` calls above; it is deliberate in Ruby** |
| `estimate_cost(input_tokens:, output_tokens:)` | `def estimate_cost(self, *, input_tokens, output_tokens)` |

Symbol values become strings: `usage_unit: :tokens` → `"tokens"`,
`:local_compute`, `:ollama_cloud_usage`; `usage_level: :medium` → `"medium"`.
The `MODELS` keys stay strings, and the inner keys (`context_window`,
`cost_per_million`, `input`, `output`, `usage_unit`, `usage_level`,
`advertised_context_window`) become string keys.

> ⚠️ **`estimate_cost` has a truthiness trap.** Ruby's
> `return nil unless input_token_cost_per_million && output_token_cost_per_million`
> is safe because **`0.0` is truthy in Ruby**, so local Ollama models (cost
> `0.0`) return a real `0.0` estimate. In Python `0.0` is **falsy**, so the
> literal translation would wrongly return `None` for every Ollama model. Write
> the guard as `if a is None or b is None: return None`. This is the single
> most likely bug in the whole step and the README's "For local Ollama models,
> token API cost is `0.0`" line is the behaviour it would break.

Division: Ruby's `/ 1_000_000.0` is float division; Python's `/` already is —
do **not** use `//`.

**`backends/anthropic.py`, `openai.py`, `gemini.py`, `ollama.py`,
`ollama_cloud.py`** ← their Ruby counterparts, one class each subclassing
`Base`. Copy the `MODELS` tables **value-for-value** (they are static tutorial
data current as of June 16, 2026 — do not "update" prices) and the `BASE_URL`
constants verbatim. Then, per backend:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `def initialize(api_key:, model:)` | `def __init__(self, *, api_key, model)` — keyword-only; then `self.api_key = api_key` and `self._configure_model(model)` |
| `Ollama#initialize(host: "http://localhost:11434", model:)` | `def __init__(self, *, model, host="http://localhost:11434")` — **reorder**: Python cannot put a defaulted parameter before a required one in the same way Ruby does. Both are keyword-only, so no call site changes |
| `case msg.role when :tool_result` | `if msg.role == "tool_result"` — roles are **strings** in Python (`Context.add_message` was already given strings in steps 01–02) |
| `when :assistant` (Gemini only) | `elif msg.role == "assistant"` |
| `msg.role.to_s` | `msg.role` — already a string |
| hash keys as symbols (`role:`, `input_schema:`) | string keys (`"role"`, `"input_schema"`) — Ruby's `JSON.pretty_generate` stringifies symbol keys anyway, so the JSON is identical |
| `tools.values.map { ... }` | `[... for tool in tools.values()]` |
| `tool.parameters.keys.map(&:to_s)` | `list(tool.parameters)` — already strings |
| `return [] if tools.empty?` (Gemini) | `if not tools: return []` |
| `"#{BASE_URL}/#{@model}:generateContent"` (Gemini) | f-string, same shape |
| `system_message + conversation` (OpenAI/Ollama/OllamaCloud) | `[{"role": "system", "content": system}] + conversation` |
| `headers` returning a hash | `def headers(self)` returning a dict with the same string keys, including `"anthropic-version": "2023-06-01"` |

Every backend keeps `to_payload(self, context, *, max_output_tokens=1024)` with
its provider's exact key set and key **order** — Anthropic
`model, system, max_tokens, tools, messages`; OpenAI `model, messages, tools,
max_completion_tokens`; Gemini `systemInstruction, contents, tools,
generationConfig`; Ollama/OllamaCloud `model, stream, messages, tools`. Python
dicts preserve insertion order and the example prints Anthropic's payload
verbatim, so **order is output-visible** for Anthropic and must match.

